WEBVTT

00:00:00.285 --> 00:00:01.877
A sales report is slow.

00:00:02.048 --> 00:00:06.047
Start with one example request and see what
it did in the database.

00:00:06.585 --> 00:00:11.094
On the Traffic page, select the service and
open the slow inbound request.

00:00:11.265 --> 00:00:16.043
This demo uses a sales report endpoint with
several PostgreSQL calls.

00:00:16.585 --> 00:00:17.933
Open the Request tab.

00:00:18.104 --> 00:00:20.566
The query count says how many statements

00:00:20.566 --> 00:00:23.520
the request ran and how many linked by
trace ID.

00:00:23.691 --> 00:00:26.120
Choose Show these queries to filter the
grid.

00:00:26.685 --> 00:00:28.070
Look at the statement text.

00:00:28.241 --> 00:00:32.389
This example loads orders and then fetches
order lines repeatedly.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.569
That's a pattern worth checking for an N+1
query.

00:00:36.119 --> 00:00:38.571
Open a statement and look for Caused by.

00:00:38.742 --> 00:00:40.976
It points back to the sales report request.

00:00:41.147 --> 00:00:43.134
A link by trace ID is exact.

00:00:43.305 --> 00:00:46.872
A link by timing can be approximate when
requests overlap.

00:00:47.419 --> 00:00:49.660
After you change the query, capture the

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.821
same route again and check how many
statements it runs.
